  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1243 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nLater this evening/tonight, Hurricane Isaias is progged to make \nlandfall over AL/MS before weakening and pivoting northwest towards \nnorthern MS Saturday afternoon, and then northeast towards the Ohio \nValley, further weakening through the remainder of the weekend. With \nthe upper ridge situated over the Central Plains, most of the \nHurricane remnants will remain out of our forecast area, with the \nexception of <20% chance of light rain over south-central MO. \n\nThrough the weekend and into the beginning of next week, the warm \nand dry airmass will continue, with highs remaining in the upper 80s \nand low 90s through Tuesday. Sunday will be the driest day, with \nminimum RHs in the 20-30% range. With the low humidity coupled with \nwinds gusting up to 20-25mph west of Highway 65, this area will \nexperience elevated fire danger Sunday afternoon/evening, meaning \nthat weather and land conditions will be favorable for erratic fire \nspread if a fire were to start. Gusty winds up to 20-30mph will \ncontinue into Monday ahead of an upper level system, with the \nelevated fire danger shifting east of Highway 65 where the lowest \nRHs (25-30%) reside. \n\nLooking back at the synoptic analysis for this weekend/next week, \nHurricane Simon will continue to progress northward over western \nMexico, with Tropical Storm Rachel progged to pivot northeast onto \nthe southern coast of CA. An upper level closed low and associated \ntrough will push down into the Pacific northwest over the weekend \nbefore ingesting Tropical Storm Rachel and pivoting over the desert \nsouthwest towards the Plains as it becomes an open wave. This is \nforecast to continue trekking towards the area, bringing our next \nchances of rainfall as early as Tuesday evening (30-60%), with the \nbest chances (45-75%) on Wednesday, continuing into Thursday (40-\n60%) before exiting the area Friday. There are still plenty of \ndiscrepancies between ensembles regarding the pattern's overall \nevolution and timing, so expect changes to the forecast over the \nnext few days. It is worth mentioning that SPC has highlighted areas \nwest of Highway 65 and north of Highway 60 in a Day 6 (Wednesday) \n15% risk for severe weather. Again, this will largely be impacted by \nhow this system evolves, so make sure to check back as the forecast \nmatures and we get a better idea of the severe risk and timing. \n\nThis system will bring a gradual cool-down, dropping highs back to \nnear-seasonal once it pushes through.",
